{"answer":"The observer is a neutral fisher chosen by the community.  Every season the fishers hold a quick vote and select one of themselves—anybody who isn’t fishing that day—to serve as the observer.  The chosen observer must be honest, have no stake in any particular fisher’s catch, and keep a separate copy of the communal ledger.  Their role is to audit the weekly catch reports, verify the 5 % deposits, and record suspensions and payments so that all fishers can see the status of each account.  If the community later feels the observer is not neutral, the next season they can vote to replace them.

The policy itself is unchanged:
"Each fisher may take no more than 15kg per trip and must contribute 5% of the catch to a community pool for restocking.  If a fisher exceeds 15kg, the excess must be returned to the lake; if they fail to record or contribute the 5%, they are suspended from fishing for one trip and must pay the value of the missed contribution.  A community observer will audit weekly.  All catch is logged on the communal ledger.  Each fisher records their catch, calculates 5% to be deposited into the community pool, and submits the deposit."","revised_policy":"Each fisher may take no more than 15kg per trip and must contribute 5% of the catch to a community pool for restocking.  If a fisher exceeds 15kg, the excess must be returned to the lake; if they fail to record or contribute the 5%, they are suspended from fishing for one trip and must pay the value of the missed contribution.  A community observer will audit weekly.  All catch is logged on the communal ledger.  Each fisher records their catch, calculates 5% to be deposited into the community pool, and submits the deposit.","revised_operationalization":"When a fisher fails to record or deposit the 5% contribution, the community observer issues a suspension notice.  The fisher cannot log a new catch or use a fishing spot until the suspension is lifted.  The fisher must pay the missed 5% value within seven days.  The observer records the suspension status and payment in the communal ledger, ensuring transparency for all fishers.","reasoning":"The observer selection does not alter the rule text, so the policy and operationalization remain as originally stated."}

{"answer":"I reckon the community needs a clear rule on what happens if we over‑catch the 20 % of the lake’s biomass. The fine should mirror the catch‑back fee so it’s easy to calculate and fair. I suggest we charge 10 % of the market value of the excess weight that caused us to breach the cap. The fee must be paid within four weeks of the meeting where the breach is announced. If a fisher can’t pay on time, the steward will put a two‑week fishing ban on that person for every 5 % of the cap that was exceeded, until the fine is settled.

With that added, the rest of my policy stays the same, but we now have a concrete fine system and enforcement step.

", "revised_policy":"Each fisher may not catch more than 20 kg per trip or 25 % of the lake’s current biomass, whichever is lower; the community’s total catch per season must not exceed 20 % of the lake’s biomass. On every trip, the fisher records catch weight and submits it to the community ledger each week. A quarterly inventory of the lake’s biomass is taken by the appointed steward. If a fisher’s catch exceeds the 20 kg limit, the excess is returned to the lake or, if the fisher chooses to keep it, the fisher must pay a catch‑back fee equal to 10 % of the market value of the excess weight. The market value for excess catch will be determined by a Community Price Committee (CPC) composed of selected fishers and the steward, using the most recent market price per kilogram for each species as reported by local fish markets. The CPC meets monthly to review prices and publishes the fee schedule at the start of each week. The steward, with input from a community committee, will decide whether the excess is returned or a catch‑back payment is required. If the cumulative catch over a season surpasses the 20 % biomass cap, the CPC and steward will calculate the excess weight and impose a fine equal to 10 % of the market value of that excess. The fine must be paid within four weeks of the community meeting that announces the breach. Failure to pay the fine will result in a temporary fishing ban of two weeks per 5 % of the cap exceeded, imposed by the steward until the fine is settled. All parties agree to this rule and will hold each other accountable through the ledger and community meetings. Season definition: A season is the calendar year from 1 January to 31 December. The 20 % biomass cap is based on the lake biomass measured by the steward on the first inventory of that year (or the most recent inventory before 1 January). The steward publishes this figure at the start of the season so all fishers know the cap. Weekly ledger submissions are checked against the 20 kg per trip and 25 % biomass limits; any excess is handled per the policy. Seasonal totals are tracked and compared to the 20 % cap, and if a threat is detected the steward calls a community meeting.", "revised_operationalization":"1. Each fisher records catch weight on the day of the trip and submits the data to the community ledger by the end of the same week. 2. The CPC reviews the latest market prices each month, updates the fee schedule, and posts it on the community notice board and the digital ledger portal. 3. When a fisher’s catch exceeds 20 kg, they consult the published fee schedule to calculate the 10 % fee on the excess weight. 4. The fisher either returns the excess weight to the lake or pays the calculated fee to the CPC within the same week. 5. The steward reviews all weekly submissions against the 20 kg per trip and 25 % biomass limits and flags any discrepancies to the CPC. 6. The CPC, with the steward’s assistance, decides on return or fee for each excess catch case. 7. Seasonal totals are accumulated weekly; the CPC and steward monitor the 20 % cap and, if exceeded, convene a community meeting to discuss bans or fines. 8. During the community meeting, the CPC and steward calculate the excess weight and impose a fine equal to 10 % of the market value of that excess. 9. The fisher must pay the fine within four weeks of the meeting. 10. If the fisher fails to pay the fine within that period, the steward imposes a temporary fishing ban of two weeks for every 5 % of the cap that was exceeded, until the fine is settled. 11. All actions are recorded in the ledger for transparency.", "reasoning":"The original policy left fines vague; by tying the fine to the 10 % catch‑back fee we keep calculations simple and proportional to the excess. Adding a clear payment window and a repeatable ban structure ensures enforcement. The rest of the policy remains unchanged to preserve the community’s established rules and monitoring procedures."}

